WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.134 --> 00:00:02.603
- Hey y'all, welcome to
this week's episode of

00:00:02.603 --> 00:00:03.503
*Is This Okay*

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:05.702
where we talk about things that
are happening in the world,

00:00:05.702 --> 00:00:07.878
wondering if it's okay.

00:00:07.878 --> 00:00:08.711
I'm Nyla.

00:00:08.711 --> 00:00:09.652
- I'm Daysha.
- I'm Chris.

00:00:09.652 --> 00:00:10.928
- I'm Nick.
- I'm Claudia.

00:00:10.928 --> 00:00:12.247
- Yay, welcome guys!

00:00:12.247 --> 00:00:13.256
- Woo!

00:00:13.256 --> 00:00:15.887
- This week our topic of discussion is

00:00:15.887 --> 00:00:17.145
white washing in Hollywood.

00:00:17.145 --> 00:00:19.874
The other night, I saw
Kubo and the Two Strings.

00:00:19.874 --> 00:00:23.208
And so, it's this beautiful
film about a Japanese family,

00:00:23.208 --> 00:00:24.756
and for whatever reason,

00:00:24.756 --> 00:00:26.179
I just had this itch where I was like,

00:00:26.179 --> 00:00:28.231
I gotta check and see who the voices are.

00:00:28.231 --> 00:00:30.276
Out of the five main characters

00:00:30.276 --> 00:00:31.669
- George Takei was the only one, right?

00:00:31.669 --> 00:00:33.459
- Yeah, was the only one who was Asian.

00:00:33.459 --> 00:00:34.560
- You kinda wonder why that

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:35.854
none of the eight people in the movie were

00:00:35.854 --> 00:00:36.687
actually Japanese.

00:00:36.687 --> 00:00:39.430
- This has been happening
for a very long time,

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:40.789
because we've seen it with Emma Stone.

00:00:40.789 --> 00:00:41.671
Still remember you.

00:00:41.671 --> 00:00:43.624
Tilda Swinton as the Ancient One,

00:00:43.624 --> 00:00:46.120
and Scarlett Johansson
as Ghost in the Shell.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.181
- There's also this
argument that, you know,

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:50.614
well, these white actors
are just more talented,

00:00:50.614 --> 00:00:53.023
and I think that that's the
argument that really irks me.

00:00:53.023 --> 00:00:54.288
- It's so insulting.

00:00:54.288 --> 00:00:57.219
- Let's say that they've
gotten more training

00:00:57.219 --> 00:01:00.128
or more schooling so that
thus makes them more talented.

00:01:00.128 --> 00:01:02.373
Why haven't we as a society said that,

00:01:02.373 --> 00:01:05.026
oh well there's this whole
other group of people

00:01:05.026 --> 00:01:07.326
that aren't getting the
training that they need

00:01:07.326 --> 00:01:09.396
to be able to compete on the same level.

00:01:09.396 --> 00:01:10.918
Let's figure out how to fix that.

00:01:10.918 --> 00:01:12.870
- This is all the funny actors
we know, and they just all

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.360
happen to be white and male and it's like,

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.509
well you're pulling from
a comedy school that needs

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:19.007
a certain level of socioeconomic status

00:01:19.007 --> 00:01:20.440
to be able to succeed in.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.569
- And also, from cultures
that don't encourage

00:01:22.569 --> 00:01:23.636
going into that industry.

00:01:23.636 --> 00:01:25.255
- Right, and then when you are

00:01:25.255 --> 00:01:27.765
very set on a certain type of comedy, too,

00:01:27.765 --> 00:01:29.502
you're not gonna get the Leslie Jones',

00:01:29.502 --> 00:01:30.995
you're not used to that type of comedy.

00:01:30.995 --> 00:01:32.812
Stars can be made, stars can be built,

00:01:32.812 --> 00:01:34.779
and studios have to commit
to finding those people.

00:01:34.779 --> 00:01:37.336
- I also just think about it
in terms of being a black woman

00:01:37.336 --> 00:01:40.847
how I could never depict
a story as a white person.

00:01:40.847 --> 00:01:42.583
That's just not my story to tell.

00:01:42.583 --> 00:01:44.300
- Can we talk about Fresh Off the Boat

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.793
and all these shows are
about the struggle of this

00:01:47.793 --> 00:01:49.402
specific type of ethnicity.

00:01:49.402 --> 00:01:51.684
I really wanna to have a
black family that's not about

00:01:51.684 --> 00:01:52.816
being black also.

00:01:52.816 --> 00:01:54.604
A show where the emotions are universal,

00:01:54.604 --> 00:01:56.225
not specific to a niche.

00:01:56.225 --> 00:01:59.655
- I think I get what you're
saying, but I also think,

00:01:59.655 --> 00:02:02.774
when you are a person of color,
your color comes with you

00:02:02.774 --> 00:02:03.732
no matter what.

00:02:03.732 --> 00:02:05.857
When you are white, you have the option

00:02:05.857 --> 00:02:07.756
to either address it or to not address it.

00:02:07.756 --> 00:02:09.679
- Well see, that seems
like the goal to me, right?

00:02:09.679 --> 00:02:10.746
Is to have the option.

00:02:10.746 --> 00:02:12.647
If you make a movie about slavery,

00:02:12.647 --> 00:02:14.015
you gotta talk about racism.

00:02:14.015 --> 00:02:16.330
If you make a movie, like
a science fiction movie

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:18.106
with Will Smith and his
son, awesome example.

00:02:18.106 --> 00:02:19.314
You don't have to talk about it

00:02:19.314 --> 00:02:20.429
because that's not what
they're dealing with.

00:02:20.429 --> 00:02:23.421
- No, and see, so I think context is key.

00:02:23.421 --> 00:02:25.468
So it's like, if you have a sci-fi movie

00:02:25.468 --> 00:02:27.896
and it's just, you happen
to have a black lead,

00:02:27.896 --> 00:02:28.729
that's totally fine.

00:02:28.729 --> 00:02:30.489
You don't necessarily
have to delve into that.

00:02:30.489 --> 00:02:31.885
But it's like, if you have a show

00:02:31.885 --> 00:02:34.204
dealing with a black family life,

00:02:34.204 --> 00:02:35.455
you know that kinda thing?

00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:36.684
I think that it would be

00:02:36.684 --> 00:02:38.039
- It would be watering it down.

00:02:38.039 --> 00:02:40.897
- Yeah, it just wouldn't be
true to the experience of

00:02:40.897 --> 00:02:41.869
my everyday life.

00:02:41.869 --> 00:02:45.099
- If you're deep diving into someone's or

00:02:45.099 --> 00:02:47.789
a group's experience in terms
of a movie or a TV show,

00:02:47.789 --> 00:02:49.416
I think it would look a
little bit more strange

00:02:49.416 --> 00:02:53.066
to not talk about those
issues or those struggles.

00:02:53.066 --> 00:02:55.618
But I also think that
what you're trying to say,

00:02:55.618 --> 00:02:58.160
there are plenty of spaces,
and we need more spaces,

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.399
where we're just allowed to be happy.

00:03:00.399 --> 00:03:01.232
- Just exist.

00:03:01.232 --> 00:03:04.017
- Ideally, you get
representation for both.

00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:06.022
You get something that
is just centered around

00:03:06.022 --> 00:03:08.649
a certain struggle and why
there needs to be a spotlight

00:03:08.649 --> 00:03:10.872
on a certain thing, and you
get the film that's just

00:03:10.872 --> 00:03:13.549
a beautiful rom-com, where
the lead is a black man

00:03:13.549 --> 00:03:16.196
or the lead is a latina woman.

00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:17.574
I guess that's Hitch, pretty much.

00:03:17.574 --> 00:03:18.407
- Right.

00:03:18.407 --> 00:03:19.240
(laughing)

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:20.117
- We got it, we did it.

00:03:20.117 --> 00:03:20.950
We fixed Hollywood.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:22.283
- We got the one.

00:03:22.283 --> 00:03:23.241
Only one.

00:03:23.241 --> 00:03:24.432
(laughs)

00:03:24.432 --> 00:03:27.123
I do think that Hollywood
definitely exploits that.

00:03:27.123 --> 00:03:29.747
So we have the struggle film,
where it's just kind of like,

00:03:29.747 --> 00:03:32.125
oh, Tyrone who grew up in the hood

00:03:32.125 --> 00:03:34.121
but he sold drugs to get out.

00:03:34.121 --> 00:03:35.384
- Didn't want his baby mama.

00:03:35.384 --> 00:03:37.945
- Uh huh, and I think that
that's just a storyline that

00:03:37.945 --> 00:03:39.438
Hollywood's like, oh it sold once

00:03:39.438 --> 00:03:41.545
during the black exploitation
era so we'll just

00:03:41.545 --> 00:03:43.488
keep repeating it over
and over and over again.

00:03:43.488 --> 00:03:45.502
And I think it goes back to that point

00:03:45.502 --> 00:03:46.754
that was brought up last week is that,

00:03:46.754 --> 00:03:48.727
money is kind of power, so it's like,

00:03:48.727 --> 00:03:51.978
we have to be thinking about,
when we do watch those films

00:03:51.978 --> 00:03:54.774
and whatnot, what are we
telling Hollywood executives

00:03:54.774 --> 00:03:56.189
that we're willing to invest in.

00:03:56.189 --> 00:03:58.911
- What's interesting also is
that most movies make their,

00:03:58.911 --> 00:04:01.268
other than comedies, most
movies make their real revenue

00:04:01.268 --> 00:04:02.379
internationally.

00:04:02.379 --> 00:04:06.004
And ones that have
international-looking stars do better.

00:04:06.004 --> 00:04:07.648
That's why the Rock is the
biggest star right now.

00:04:07.648 --> 00:04:09.979
- Unfortunately, I think
there's so much truth in that,

00:04:09.979 --> 00:04:13.448
but it takes people in
power, literally just making

00:04:13.448 --> 00:04:16.341
good moral decisions
that help other people.

00:04:16.341 --> 00:04:17.874
And it's like, how do
you tell that to somebody

00:04:17.874 --> 00:04:19.649
when all the see is dollar signs.

00:04:19.649 --> 00:04:22.063
- Nick, I have a question
for you as an actor.

00:04:22.063 --> 00:04:24.110
If a studio tomorrow was like,

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:25.959
"hey we have this really
big blockbuster film,

00:04:25.959 --> 00:04:27.641
"we've seen your work, we love it,

00:04:27.641 --> 00:04:30.336
"but we want you to play X race."

00:04:30.336 --> 00:04:32.216
- We want you to play samurai
- Put him on the hot seat

00:04:32.216 --> 00:04:33.317
- So nervous right now.

00:04:33.317 --> 00:04:34.525
- No judgement.

00:04:34.525 --> 00:04:36.104
- Thank you very much.
- Yeah, no judgement.

00:04:36.104 --> 00:04:38.237
- Just to be fair before you answer,

00:04:38.237 --> 00:04:39.667
if I can offer any support.

00:04:39.667 --> 00:04:42.052
I have struggled with that
idea too in a sense of,

00:04:42.052 --> 00:04:43.459
if out of no where I was offered

00:04:43.459 --> 00:04:46.252
a role in a Woody Allen film,
and that was my big break.

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:47.755
And I have all these thoughts of like,

00:04:47.755 --> 00:04:49.329
I do not know what that is about

00:04:49.329 --> 00:04:51.390
and I do judge actors
that take roles in that,

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:53.307
but I also don't know their circumstance.

00:04:53.307 --> 00:04:54.654
It's a really tricky question.

00:04:54.654 --> 00:04:56.002
- It's a weird thing as an actor,

00:04:56.002 --> 00:04:57.606
you get used to not turning down roles,

00:04:57.606 --> 00:04:59.413
especially if you're an aspiring actor.

00:04:59.413 --> 00:05:03.268
So if I, or anybody else that
I know, is offered a role

00:05:03.268 --> 00:05:05.593
that they think they can do something with

00:05:05.593 --> 00:05:06.942
then yeah, I think they should take it.

00:05:06.942 --> 00:05:09.887
I don't like shaming actors
that apologize afterwards.

00:05:09.887 --> 00:05:12.465
- But I think part of
it is the authenticity

00:05:12.465 --> 00:05:13.454
of the apology

00:05:13.454 --> 00:05:15.906
and feeling like you know
this person is actually

00:05:15.906 --> 00:05:17.074
learning from their mistakes.

00:05:17.074 --> 00:05:19.907
- I see a lot of people that fuck up,

00:05:19.907 --> 00:05:21.027
make apologies that like,

00:05:21.027 --> 00:05:22.942
"I really hope that this
sparks more conversation

00:05:22.942 --> 00:05:24.734
"about the issue" and I'm like,

00:05:24.734 --> 00:05:26.464
"we've been talking about this.

00:05:26.464 --> 00:05:28.273
"There are no cookies for you for

00:05:28.273 --> 00:05:29.886
"making everyone pissed about this."

00:05:29.886 --> 00:05:30.969
- No cookies for you.

00:05:30.969 --> 00:05:32.693
- Whenever I see those
apologies, I'm like,

00:05:32.693 --> 00:05:35.246
"would that fly with
your husband or wife?"

00:05:35.246 --> 00:05:39.111
- All of this is why I am
participating so heavily in

00:05:39.111 --> 00:05:42.350
new media, because I think this
new media has become a very

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:45.148
blooming space for people of
color to tell their stories

00:05:45.148 --> 00:05:46.720
because it's all on their own terms.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:49.976
- So, I think we've come
down to a conclusion

00:05:49.976 --> 00:05:52.531
that white washing is a real thing,

00:05:52.531 --> 00:05:54.142
and it's just not,

00:05:54.142 --> 00:05:55.093
not cool.

00:05:55.093 --> 00:05:55.926
- It's not okay.
- Yeah.

00:05:55.926 --> 00:05:56.759
- It's not okay.

00:05:56.759 --> 00:05:57.592
- Not okay.

00:05:57.592 --> 00:06:01.316
So leave your comments below,
and we will see you next week.

00:06:01.316 --> 00:06:02.316
- [All] Bye.

00:06:04.562 --> 00:06:07.062
(funky music)

